export function createUsageMeter(limit: number): UsageMeter;
```

`limit` must be a positive safe integer; otherwise the factory throws `RangeError`. Each `units` argument must be a non-negative safe integer; otherwise the method throws `RangeError`.

A new meter starts with zero used units. `consume` accepts as many requested units as fit, increases usage by that accepted amount, and returns the accepted amount. `release` removes at most the currently used amount and returns the amount actually released. `remaining` returns `limit - used`. `utilization` returns `used / limit`.

Every meter created by the factory must own independent state: operations on one meter must never change the observations or future results of another meter.

Use only deterministic, standard-library TypeScript. Do not use filesystem access, network access, clocks, randomness, external packages, or frameworks.","reference_code":"export interface UsageMeter {
  consume(units: number): number;
  release(units: number): number;
  used(): number;
  remaining(): number;
  utilization(): number;
}

export function createUsageMeter(limit: number): UsageMeter {
  if (!Number.isSafeInteger(limit) || limit <= 0) {
    throw new RangeError('limit must be a positive safe integer');
  }

  let usedUnits = 0;

  const validateUnits = (units: number): void => {
    if (!Number.isSafeInteger(units) || units < 0) {
      throw new RangeError('units must be a non-negative safe integer');
    }
  };

  return {
    consume(units: number): number {
      validateUnits(units);
      const accepted = Math.min(units, limit - usedUnits);
      usedUnits += accepted;
